# Level 0 → Level 1

## Objective

Connect to the Bandit server through SSH and retrieve the password stored in the provided file.

## Approach

The first level introduced the basic workflow:

```bash
ssh bandit0@<host> -p <port>

After connecting, I inspected the current directory and read the relevant file.

Useful commands:

pwd
ls
cat readme
What I learned
SSH basics
Remote Linux environments
Basic file navigation
Reading files from the command line
Level 1 → Level 2
Objective

Retrieve the password from a file whose name begins with -.

Approach

A filename beginning with - can be interpreted by commands as an option rather than a filename.

This required explicitly identifying the file.

Example concept:

cat ./-
What I learned

Linux commands can interpret certain filenames as command-line options.

Using:

./filename

can make it clear that the argument refers to a file.

Level 2 → Level 3
Objective

Read a file whose name contains spaces.

Approach

The filename had to be treated as a single argument.

Techniques include:

cat "filename with spaces"

or escaping spaces.

cat filename\ with\ spaces
What I learned

Shells use spaces to separate arguments, so filenames containing spaces need to be quoted or escaped.

Level 3 → Level 4
Objective

Find a hidden file inside the inhere directory.

Approach

I used:

cd inhere
ls -la

The -a option displays hidden files.

The relevant hidden file could then be read with:

cat <hidden-file>
What I learned

Hidden files in Linux commonly begin with . and are not displayed by a normal ls.

Level 4 → Level 5
Objective

Identify the human-readable file among multiple files.

Approach

I examined the files and used the file command to determine their types.

file ./*

The relevant file could then be read.

cat <file>
What I learned

The file command is useful during enumeration because the file extension does not necessarily tell you what type of data a file contains.

Level 5 → Level 6
Objective

Find a file based on specific properties including:

Human-readable
Specific file size
Non-executable
Approach

I used find to narrow down the candidates.

find inhere -type f -size 1033c

I then verified the candidate using:

file <file>

and read the contents.

What I learned

Instead of manually checking every file, Linux find can search based on properties such as:

File type
Size
Name
Permissions
Ownership

This was an important introduction to systematic enumeration.

Level 6 → Level 7
Objective

Find a file somewhere on the system based on:

Owner
Group
File size
Approach

I used:

find / -type f -user bandit7 -group bandit6 -size 33c 2>/dev/null

The search identified the relevant file under:

/var/lib/dpkg/info/

The contents were then read using:

cat <file>
Important Concepts
-user

Searches for files owned by a particular user.

-group

Searches for files belonging to a particular group.

-size

Filters based on file size.

2>/dev/null

Redirects error output so that permission-denied messages do not clutter the results.

What I learned

This level was particularly useful for understanding how multiple enumeration conditions can be combined into a single search.

Level 7 → Level 8
Objective

Find the password inside a large file based on a specific keyword.

Approach

Instead of manually reading the entire file, I used text searching.

grep <keyword> data.txt
What I learned

grep is extremely useful when searching large amounts of text.

Instead of:

Read everything
      ↓
Look manually

I can use:

File
 ↓
grep
 ↓
Relevant line
Level 8 → Level 9
Objective

Identify the line that occurs only once in a file.

Approach

I used text-processing commands such as:

sort data.txt | uniq -u
Concepts
sort

Sorts the input.

uniq

Filters repeated lines.

uniq -u

Displays lines that occur only once.

What I learned

Combining Linux commands using pipes allows multiple processing stages to be chained together.

Level 9 → Level 10
Objective

Find readable information among binary/non-readable data.

Approach

I used:

strings data.txt

and searched the resulting output for useful information.

What I learned

strings extracts sequences of printable characters from binary or otherwise non-text data.

This is useful during basic binary and file enumeration.

Level 10 → Level 11
Objective

Decode Base64-encoded data.

Approach

I identified the encoded content and decoded it using a Base64 decoder.

Example:

base64 -d
What I learned

Encoding is not the same as encryption.

Base64 is an encoding mechanism used to represent binary/text data using a restricted character set.

Level 11 → Level 12
Objective

Decode text that had been transformed using a rotational substitution.

Approach

The challenge required identifying the transformation and reversing it.

I used Linux tools and/or appropriate character transformation techniques to recover the original text.

A useful command for this type of transformation is:

tr
What I learned

Not every transformed string is encrypted.

Recognizing common encoding and substitution patterns is useful during security investigations and CTFs.

Level 12 → Level 13
Objective

Recover the password from a file that had been repeatedly compressed and archived using different formats.

Approach

This challenge required identifying the file type at each stage and repeatedly extracting/decompressing it.

The general methodology was:

Compressed / archived file
        ↓
Identify format
        ↓
Decompress / extract
        ↓
Identify new format
        ↓
Repeat
        ↓
Recover readable data

Useful tools/concepts included:

file
mv
gzip
gunzip
bzip2
bunzip2
tar
What I learned

The important lesson was not memorizing one command.

The process was:

Identify the current file format.
Rename the file if necessary.
Use the appropriate extraction/decompression tool.
Inspect the resulting file.
Repeat until the underlying data is recovered.

This introduced a practical approach to analyzing layered or disguised files.

Level 13 → Level 14
Objective

Use an SSH private key to authenticate to the next Bandit level.

Approach

The challenge introduced SSH private-key authentication.

The general process involved:

Private key
     ↓
Correct permissions
     ↓
SSH authentication
     ↓
Next Bandit account

The private key file needed appropriate permissions before being used.

Example:

chmod 600 <private-key>

Then SSH can use the key for authentication.

What I Learned

SSH authentication does not always require a password.

It can also use public-key cryptography:

Private Key → Client
Public Key  → Server

The private key must be protected because possession of it may allow authentication as the associated account.

